@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# 9. Due times for response-time promises
+
+Date: 2025-10-06
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The help desk is promising a response time by priority. Agents need to see when each ticket is due and to list the ones
+that have slipped. The hours will change with staffing, without a release. Tickets already in production have no due
+time. If the new release misbehaves, operators must be able to go back to 1.4.0 on the data file the new release wrote
+and still see every ticket.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will store a `due` time on each ticket: the time it was created plus the configured hours for its priority (4 / 24 /
+72 / 168 for urgent / high / normal / low, overridable by `DUE_HOURS_*`). The time is written once and never recomputed,
+so a later change of the hours or of the ticket's priority leaves it where it was.
+
+Tickets saved without `due` receive one at load from their `created` time and current priority, and keep it from then
+on. The data file stays at version 1; 1.4.0 ignores the extra field, so a rollback still starts and shows every ticket.
+
+`DUE_TIMES=on` is the switch that shows `due` on tickets and honours `GET /tickets?overdue=true` (open and in-progress
+tickets whose due time has passed). Until then the API is unchanged, so production can write due times into the data
+file first and only then turn the feature on.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Operators change the hours with environment variables, not a code change.
+- Rolling back to 1.4.0 drops `due` from the running process (the field is unknown to it) but keeps every ticket.
+- `src/tickets/due.js` is the one place that knows how due times are computed and when a ticket is overdue.
