@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# 9. Ticket assignment
+
+Date: 2025-10-20
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Open tickets sat in the queue with no one responsible for them. Operators need to put a ticket on an agent's list, take
+it off again, and list a person's work — without changing the lifecycle.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will record the agent a ticket is assigned to on the ticket itself.
+
+- `PUT /tickets/:id/assignee` with `{ "assignee": "<agent>" }` assigns the ticket; `{ "assignee": null }` unassigns it.
+  An agent is a non-empty name of at most 64 characters.
+- Only open and in-progress tickets can be assigned or unassigned. Assigning a ticket to the agent it already has is
+  not a change.
+- Every ticket includes `assignee` (`null` when unassigned). Files written before this field load it as null.
+- `GET /tickets` filters by `assignee`; empty `assignee=` is the unassigned tickets. A ticket must match every filter
+  given.
+- The assigned agent is notified through the outbox (`ticket.assigned`). The command-line listing shows the assignee.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Assignment is a store change like any other: one audit entry (`ticket.assigned`), one save, and a notification when
+  the assignee is an agent.
+- Resolved and closed tickets keep the assignee they had; they cannot be reassigned until reopened.
+- `list --raw` keeps id, status, priority and title in the first four columns, and appends assignee after them.
