+## Due times
+
+Response-time due times are stored on every ticket (`due`) as soon as this release starts, including tickets that were
+already in the data file (from their `created` time and priority). The API does not show them, and does not honour
+`?overdue=true`, until `DUE_TIMES=on`.
+
+Roll-out:
+
+1. Deploy this release with `DUE_TIMES` unset. Confirm each ticket in the data file has a `due` field.
+2. Set `DUE_TIMES=on` and reload the process.
+3. If the release misbehaves, go back to 1.4.0 on the same data file: the file is still version 1, 1.4.0 ignores `due`,
+   and every ticket still loads.
+
+The hours (4 / 24 / 72 / 168 for urgent / high / normal / low) are `DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`,
+`DUE_HOURS_NORMAL` and `DUE_HOURS_LOW`. Changing them does not move a ticket that already has a due time.
+
 ## The command line and the weekly report
 
 `bin/tickets.js` and `scripts/weekly-report.js` read the data file and never write it, so they are safe to run beside
